==Latvian==
{{slim-wikipedia|lang=lv}}
[[File:1080PoisonWarning gobeirne.jpg|thumb|250px|Inde (etiķete)]]

===Etymology===
A 20th-century [[neologism]], introduced in the Scientific Terminology Dictionary (Riga, 1922) to replace a previous [[Germanism]], {{term|ģifts|lang=lv}}. The word was coined by shortening the (old-fashioned, dialectal) word {{term|indeve||illness, disease; bad disposition; evil spirit; poison|lang=lv}}, which [[w:Jānis Endzelīns|J. Endzelīns]] considered either an old [[w:en:Curonian language|Curonian]] term or a [[borrowing]] from {{etyl|lt|-}} ([[cf]]. {{etyl|lt|-}} dialectal {{term|indėvė||poison; evil, evil spirit|lang=lt}}), perhaps formed from a prefix {{term/t|lv|*in-}} ({{etyl|lv|-}} {{term|ie-|lang=lv}}) and the verb {{term|dot||to give|lang=lv}} or {{term|dēt||to lay (eggs); orig. to put|lang=lv}}. The meaning evolution would be similar to that of {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Gift|lang=de}}: from “something given, put (in)” to “poison.” Another possiblity, suggested by the “evil spirit” meaning of the {{etyl|lt|-}} cognate (also attested in older {{etyl|lv|-}} sources as a name for the devil), is that {{term|indeve|lang=lv}} might come from {{term/t|lv|*in-||negative}} + {{term|dievs|lang=lv}}, i.e. “no-god” > “evil, evil spirit” ([[cf]]. similarly formed {{term|nedievs|lang=lv}}). It is also possible that two similar words, meaning “disease” and “evil spirit,” became homophonous and merged as {{term|indeve|lang=lv}}. It has also been suggested that {{etyl|dum|-}} {{term|inde||end; death|lang=dum}}, {{term|inden||to end life, to die|lang=dum}} could also have influenced {{term|indeve|lang=lv}}, given the strong presence of Dutch sailors and craftsmen in the times of the old [[w:en:Duchy of Courland and Semigallia|Duchy of Courland]] (1561-1726).<ref>{{R:lv:LEV}}</ref>

